  "live_records": {
    "q-338485": {
      "dom_id": "step-q338485",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3x+25}}{x}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3x+25}}{x} & =\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3(0)+25}}{0}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3x+25}}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(5-\\sqrt{3x+25})(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})}{x(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{3x+25})}^{2}}{x(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{25-(3x+25)}{x(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3x}{x(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3}{5+\\sqrt{3x+25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{5+\\sqrt{3(0)+25}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
