    "q-338468": {
      "dom_id": "step-q338468",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{x-1}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{x-1} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1}-1}{1-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-1)(\\sqrt{x}+1)}{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{1}^{2}}{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\cancel{x-1}}{\\cancel{(x-1)}(\\sqrt{x}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
