    "q-46832": {
      "dom_id": "step-q46832",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{8-4\\sqrt{x}}{x-4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{8-4\\sqrt{x}}{x-4} & =\\frac{8-4\\sqrt{4}}{4-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{8-4\\sqrt{x}}{x-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{4(2-\\sqrt{x})}{x-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{4(2-\\sqrt{x})(2+\\sqrt{x})}{(x-4)(2+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{4({2}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2})}{(x-4)(2+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{4(4-x)}{(x-4)(2+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{-4(x-4)}{(x-4)(2+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{-4}{2+\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{2+\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
