    "q-338484": {
      "dom_id": "step-q338484",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-x-6}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-x-6} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3+1}-2}{{3}^{2}-3-6}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-x-6} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1}-2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{({x}^{2}-x-6)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1})^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x+2)(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x+1)-4}{(x+2)(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{(x+2)(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{1}{(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(3+2)(\\sqrt{3+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
