    "q-4467": {
      "dom_id": "step-q4467",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5}} & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{0+5}-\\sqrt{5}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{(\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5})(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{{(\\sqrt{x+5})}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{x+5-5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+5}+\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
