      "dom_id": "step-q338523",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}-\\sqrt{4x+1}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=1,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}-\\sqrt{4x+1}} & =\\frac{1-1}{\\sqrt{3(1)+2}-\\sqrt{4(1)+1}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}-\\sqrt{4x+1}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})}{(\\sqrt{3x+2}-\\sqrt{4x+1})(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})}{(\\sqrt{3x+2})^{2}-(\\sqrt{4x+1})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})}{(3x+2)-(4x+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-(1-x)(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})}{1-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}[-(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})] \\\\  & =-(\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
