    "q-46840": {
      "dom_id": "step-q46840",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{1-\\sqrt{x}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=1$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{1-\\sqrt{x}} & =\\frac{1-{1}^{2}}{1-\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{1-\\sqrt{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{(1-{x}^{2})(1+\\sqrt{x})}{(1-\\sqrt{x})(1+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{(1-x)(1+x)(1+\\sqrt{x})}{({1}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{(1-x)(1+x)(1+\\sqrt{x})}{(1-x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(1+x)(1+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =(1+1)(1+\\sqrt{1}) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
